    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d44">

                <pb facs="0288" n="137" xml:id="pg137"/>

                <head>44. Telegram 1171 from Paris, September 1<anchor n="1" xml:id="d44-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Paris</placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-head" calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1961-09-01T12:00:00+01:00">September 1, 1961,
                        noon</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <pb facs="d44-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d44-1"/>
                <p>In view of press reports suggesting imminence US counter-decision to resume
                    testing, from vantage point here it would seem advisable to draw maximum
                    benefits from Soviet-Communist embarrassment and relatively favorable position
                        US-UK have enjoyed for past several months
                    to refrain from immediate decision (or at least announcement decision) to resume
                    testing. In view concern various parts world over testing, including exaggerated
                    concern over health effects noticeable unsophisticated countries, we wonder if
                    it would not be wise to play to maximum this situation in light forthcoming
                    Belgrade conference non-engaged states and General Assembly consideration
                    US-inscribed topic for next two months before any decision announced US follow
                    suit further atomic tests.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Gavin</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d44-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d44-f1-content">
                <p>Views on refraining from decision or announcement to resume testing.
                    Confidential. 1 p. Department of State, Central Files, 711.5611/9–161.</p>
            </note>
